WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.380
Instructor: So this section is focused

00:01.380 --> 00:04.830
on getting code down from GitHub to our machine.

00:04.830 --> 00:06.870
And this is really important when we're collaborating

00:06.870 --> 00:09.450
with others because those other people

00:09.450 --> 00:11.520
will be changing our repositories,

00:11.520 --> 00:13.470
making new commits, pushing those up.

00:13.470 --> 00:15.510
How do we get those down?

00:15.510 --> 00:17.550
So before we talk about the different commands,

00:17.550 --> 00:19.110
git fetch and git pull,

00:19.110 --> 00:20.670
we have to understand a little bit more

00:20.670 --> 00:24.750
about how cloning a repo works and how remotes work.

00:24.750 --> 00:28.260
So when we clone a repository, whatever the repository is

00:28.260 --> 00:31.290
whether it's one I made or I'm cloning React

00:31.290 --> 00:34.320
or some really popular open source repository

00:34.320 --> 00:36.150
what happens is that, you know

00:36.150 --> 00:39.330
we have at the very beginning nothing on my machine.

00:39.330 --> 00:41.160
We have the GitHub repository.

00:41.160 --> 00:43.560
Let's just say it's three commits, one branch

00:43.560 --> 00:44.880
the master branch.

00:44.880 --> 00:48.690
When I clone that, of course git clone this URL

00:48.690 --> 00:51.030
I end up with the three commits,

00:51.030 --> 00:53.010
all files from the repository.

00:53.010 --> 00:54.990
We have a git repo, right?

00:54.990 --> 00:57.000
We didn't have one before, now we do.

00:57.000 --> 00:59.100
And I have the master branch

00:59.100 --> 01:02.490
but there's actually two branch references here

01:02.490 --> 01:04.680
and we've only talked about one.

01:04.680 --> 01:07.620
So there is the standard master branch reference,

01:07.620 --> 01:09.770
a regular old branch reference.

01:09.770 --> 01:12.840
And I should mention, you know, this uses master

01:12.840 --> 01:15.210
but whatever the default branch is,

01:15.210 --> 01:17.040
when we clone, that's what we end up with.

01:17.040 --> 01:22.040
So whether it's main or master or trunk or purple chicken,

01:22.230 --> 01:23.130
it doesn't matter.

01:23.130 --> 01:26.520
Whatever that GitHub repo has as its default branch

01:26.520 --> 01:29.010
that's what we're gonna start with when we clone.

01:29.010 --> 01:31.020
Okay, so in this diagram, it's master.

01:31.020 --> 01:33.600
We have our regular branch reference

01:33.600 --> 01:36.030
and that acts like any other branch reference.

01:36.030 --> 01:38.790
If we add new commits, it will move.

01:38.790 --> 01:43.170
But then there's this other thing, origin slash master.

01:43.170 --> 01:47.040
This is something called a remote tracking branch reference.

01:47.040 --> 01:50.220
It sounds complicated, but basically it's just a pointer

01:50.220 --> 01:52.380
in the same way that this is a pointer

01:52.380 --> 01:55.890
but this pointer does not move, or I don't move it myself.

01:55.890 --> 01:58.020
It's pointing back to the last known commit

01:58.020 --> 02:01.230
on the master branch from the origin remote.

02:01.230 --> 02:03.960
So in other words, wherever this is pointing,

02:03.960 --> 02:05.460
just like any other branch reference,

02:05.460 --> 02:08.280
I like to visualize them as a repo,

02:08.280 --> 02:11.130
as a big book with a bunch of bookmarks in there.

02:11.130 --> 02:14.520
So these two bookmarks will start at the same spot

02:14.520 --> 02:17.340
but they might diverge as I do more work

02:17.340 --> 02:20.430
and make commits on the master branch locally.

02:20.430 --> 02:24.000
So to reiterate this term remote tracking branch,

02:24.000 --> 02:26.880
think of it as the bookmark or a pointer

02:26.880 --> 02:29.670
that remembers at the last time you communicated

02:29.670 --> 02:31.380
with this remote repo.

02:31.380 --> 02:34.200
Here is where the master branch or the main branch

02:34.200 --> 02:36.360
or the purple branch was pointing

02:36.360 --> 02:39.090
on that GitHub repo on origin.

02:39.090 --> 02:42.390
So they followed this pattern remote slash branch name.

02:42.390 --> 02:44.400
So Git names them for us.

02:44.400 --> 02:47.250
As we talked about, origin is a very common remote name.

02:47.250 --> 02:49.470
So we've got origin master

02:49.470 --> 02:53.880
but that might as well be origin, eggplant or upstream.

02:53.880 --> 02:56.370
That's another common remote name that we'll talk about.

02:56.370 --> 02:59.340
And then Logo Design, that might be a branch.

02:59.340 --> 03:02.520
It could be any remote name slash a branch.

03:02.520 --> 03:06.480
But just as the example, if we clone a repo,

03:06.480 --> 03:07.980
by default when we clone,

03:07.980 --> 03:10.410
the remote name will be set to origin

03:10.410 --> 03:12.060
and whatever that default branch name

03:12.060 --> 03:15.780
in this case master will give us origin master.

03:15.780 --> 03:18.330
So we can actually view these remote tracking branches.

03:18.330 --> 03:21.570
If we use git branch with dash R

03:21.570 --> 03:23.520
we can see that branch reference.

03:23.520 --> 03:25.230
So I'm gonna show you that now.

03:25.230 --> 03:26.880
So I'll make a new repository

03:26.880 --> 03:28.350
once again just to demonstrate this.

03:28.350 --> 03:32.450
I'll call this animals and I'll make it public.

03:32.450 --> 03:33.840
I won't add any files

03:33.840 --> 03:36.420
so I'll just have this Animals repository

03:36.420 --> 03:38.520
and I'm gonna do something slightly different

03:38.520 --> 03:39.840
that we haven't seen before.

03:39.840 --> 03:42.690
I'm actually gonna create a new file on GitHub

03:42.690 --> 03:45.780
so that when I clone it, I get some information

03:45.780 --> 03:48.210
along with, you know, the initial state of my repo.

03:48.210 --> 03:49.710
I'll actually have some files

03:49.710 --> 03:53.010
so I can make changes and make commits on GitHub.

03:53.010 --> 03:57.840
So my Animals project, I'll have a file called pets.txt.

03:57.840 --> 03:59.910
And then in here, I'm just gonna keep it really simple.

03:59.910 --> 04:01.620
I'll add some of my pets over the years.

04:01.620 --> 04:05.700
I've got Rusty and Blue, I've got a cat named Scout.

04:05.700 --> 04:08.490
She also goes by the name of Rocket

04:08.490 --> 04:10.110
and I've got some chickens.

04:10.110 --> 04:12.599
Okay, so whatever I have in this file

04:12.599 --> 04:14.520
I then need to make a commit

04:14.520 --> 04:17.400
just like I would locally on my machine.

04:17.400 --> 04:19.200
I'll just leave it as the default here.

04:19.200 --> 04:23.760
Create pets.txt, we make that commit, great.

04:23.760 --> 04:27.900
And now we have a file in my repository

04:27.900 --> 04:30.150
and we have one commit.

04:30.150 --> 04:32.910
And remember in GitHub, on GitHub

04:32.910 --> 04:34.680
the default branch name is main.

04:34.680 --> 04:36.810
If I don't push up a branch first

04:36.810 --> 04:39.120
if I don't rename it, we'll go with main.

04:39.120 --> 04:41.010
Okay, so I'm gonna clone this now.

04:41.010 --> 04:43.590
I'll copy this URL like we've done before.

04:43.590 --> 04:45.060
And then I have a new empty folder.

04:45.060 --> 04:46.530
So you can see on my computer

04:46.530 --> 04:49.980
I'm not in a repo and I'm gonna clone.

04:49.980 --> 04:53.100
So git clone that animals URL.

04:53.100 --> 04:55.560
So we now have this animals directory

04:55.560 --> 05:00.120
and if I type git branch, we have one branch main.

05:00.120 --> 05:03.390
But as we talked about when I clone something

05:03.390 --> 05:05.490
we automatically get this remote, right?

05:05.490 --> 05:08.610
Get remote dash V, it's called origin.

05:08.610 --> 05:11.100
And then the one branch we have is called main.

05:11.100 --> 05:13.980
But Git sets up a remote tracking branch

05:13.980 --> 05:16.530
for this main branch on origin.

05:16.530 --> 05:18.090
It's this little reminder here

05:18.090 --> 05:20.880
in this case origin master, but in our case, origin main.

05:20.880 --> 05:22.410
But it's a remote tracking branch

05:22.410 --> 05:25.140
a bookmark that holds the spot.

05:25.140 --> 05:27.510
The most recently known state

05:27.510 --> 05:29.460
of the master branch on origin,

05:29.460 --> 05:32.400
or in our case of the main branch on origin.

05:32.400 --> 05:35.670
So right now, if I take a look, remember I can do this.

05:35.670 --> 05:40.440
Git branch dash R, I can see this origin slash main.

05:40.440 --> 05:42.600
So the main branch on origin

05:42.600 --> 05:45.660
that is the remote tracking branch.

05:45.660 --> 05:48.150
So currently if we were to diagram this

05:48.150 --> 05:50.700
we have two branch references pointing at the same commit

05:50.700 --> 05:52.470
because I just cloned.

05:52.470 --> 05:53.850
But in the next video we'll see

05:53.850 --> 05:55.950
that we can get them to diverge

05:55.950 --> 05:59.073
and we can check one out and see the differences.
